    {
      "id": "s19",
      "type": "RECAP",
      "narration": "So that's uv, end to end. uv init lays down the project. Then uv run builds the environment while it runs your code. Dependencies go in and out with uv add and uv remove, and the lockfile records what you got. uvx borrows a tool for one run, while uv tool install keeps one around. Interpreters come from uv python, and uv build makes the files PyPI wants. One binary, and it replaced every tool we opened with.",
      "transition": "slide",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "data": {
        "heading": "uv, end to end",
        "points": [
          {
            "text": "uv init — the project on disk",
            "atWord": 6.083
          },
          {
            "text": "uv run — builds .venv as it runs",
            "atWord": 12.083
          },
          {
            "text": "uv add / remove — dependencies",
            "atWord": 25.917
          },
          {
            "text": "uvx / uv tool install — tools",
            "atWord": 35.417
          },
          {
            "text": "uv build — sdist and wheel",
            "atWord": 53.583
          }
        ]
